# Line endings are part of an artefact's bytes.
#
# This repository is worked on from Windows locally and from Ubuntu cloud
# sessions, and SPEC's third invariant asks for byte-identical output. Left to
# each machine's `core.autocrlf`, a line ending becomes a property of whoever
# ran the command: this checkout currently holds `store.py` as CRLF and
# `README.md` as LF, in the same working tree, for no reason anybody chose.
# Normalise in the repository and on checkout, on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two artefacts this project persists. `EmpiricalTable.save` and
# `TranscriptStore.save` both pin `newline="\n"` at the writer, so these are
# already LF before git sees them; the rule is here so that stays true if a
# future writer forgets, and so the files are never rewritten on checkout.
#
# Deliberately `text`, not `-text`. Marking them binary would stop git
# converting them and also stop it diffing them, and a recorded transcript is
# sorted and indented precisely so that it reviews as a diff.
*.json text eol=lf

# Frozen. Reformatting it is what `extend-exclude = ["docs"]` stops ruff doing;
# this stops git doing the same thing by a different route.
docs/SPEC.md text eol=lf

# The Hypothesis corpus (gate A33). `-text`, and here the reasoning inverts the
# one given for `*.json` above.
#
# These are opaque binary written by `choices_to_bytes`, not an artefact anybody
# reads. Under the repo-wide `text=auto` git *guesses*: an entry that happens to
# contain no NUL byte is judged text, and `\r\n` inside it is rewritten to `\n`
# on the way into the object store. Demonstrated on this tree --
# `A\r\nBC\r\nDE` hashes to d1ae50da raw and 175dd737 through the filter, and
# comes back out as `A\nBC\nDE`.
#
# The consequence is worse than a corrupt file, because nothing reports it:
# `choices_from_bytes` rejects the mangled entry and Hypothesis then **deletes**
# it from the database. The counterexample would vanish silently on the machine
# that pulled it, which is the exact failure the committed corpus exists to
# prevent. Losing the diff is no loss here -- unlike a recorded transcript,
# these were never reviewable.
tests/regressions/** -text

# ...except the directory's own README, which is hand-written prose and wants
# the repository-wide rule like any other document. The pattern above is a
# recursive wildcard and does not distinguish them, so the exception has to be
# spelled out and has to come after it. Without this line the one file in there
# that a person actually edits is the one file exempt from normalisation.
tests/regressions/README.md text eol=lf
